      "trend": "The trend in the research is to develop high-entropy alloys (HEAs) with improved mechanical properties, such as strength, hardness, and ductility, while also enhancing their thermal stability, corrosion resistance, and radiation damage resistance. Various processing techniques, including selective laser melting (SLM), laser powder bed fusion (LPBF), and spark plasma sintering (SPS), have been employed to fabricate HEAs with optimized microstructures and properties. The addition of alloying elements, such as Cr, V, and Hf, has been explored to improve the properties of HEAs. Furthermore, the evolution of chemical short-range order (SRO) and the formation of ordered clusters have been investigated to understand their effects on the lattice thermal conductivity of HEAs.",
      "unsolved": "One of the remaining challenges is to achieve a balance between the strength, ductility, and thermal stability of HEAs, as well as to understand the underlying mechanisms that control their properties. Additionally, the development of HEAs with superior radiation damage resistance and the investigation of their behavior under extreme irradiation environments are still ongoing research areas.",
